#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228946 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "89706c7f-b9a4-5d5d-84e8-2c6df19db4ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-21860" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ if \n\n$$\ny' - 3y = 2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_{\\pi/2}(t)=u\\!\\left(t-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\sin\\omega t\\} = \\dfrac{\\omega}{s^2+\\omega^2}, \\qquad s > 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}-3y} & =L{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}-3L{y} & =L{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\cdot sin\\,(2t-\\pi )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $\\mathcal L\\left\\{y' \\right\\},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{y' \\right\\}  = sY(s) - y(0)   = sY(s) + 2\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n2u_{\\pi/2}(t)\\cdot \\sin\\!\\left(2t-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $f(t)=2\\sin(2t-\\pi).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)\\} = e^{-as} \\cdot \\mathcal L\\{f(t+a)\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )} & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L{2sin\\,(2\\,(t+\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi )} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot L{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(\\pi /2)s}\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}-3L{y} & =L{2{u}_{\\pi /2}(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-\\pi )} \\\\ (sY(s)+2)-3Y(s) & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s)+2 & =\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ (s-3)Y(s) & =-2+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{{s}^{2}+4} \\\\ Y(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{4{e}^{-\\pi s/2}}{({s}^{2}+4)(s-3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228947 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"}] :question/id #uuid "07f59403-66ac-531c-81d3-e286231595ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-21861" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 4y = u_3(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,, \\qquad y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere $u_3(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s) = \\mathcal L\\{y(t)\\}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s) = -\\dfrac{2}{s+4} + \\dfrac{e^{-3s}}{s^2(s+4)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential $e^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+4)$ and a repeated linear factor $s^2,$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{s^2(s+4)} = \\dfrac{A}{s} + \\dfrac{B}{s^2} + \\dfrac{C}{s+4}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1 = As(s+4) + B(s+4) + Cs^2.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A, B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(4) + C(0) \\implies B = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n1 = A(0) + B(0) + C(16) \\implies C = \\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of $s^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 = A + C \\implies A = -C = -\\dfrac{1}{16}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s) = -\\dfrac{1}{16s} + \\dfrac{1}{4s^2} + \\dfrac{1}{16(s+4)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t) = \\mathcal L^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{16s}+\\frac{1}{4{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{16(s+4)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the $e^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\ne^{-as}\\cdot F(s)  = \\mathcal L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{-1} \\{ e^{-as}F(s) \\} = u(t-a) \\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)[-\\frac{1}{16}+\\frac{t-3}{4}+\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}] \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{-4(t-3)}+\\frac{4t-13}{16}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t) = -2e^{-4t} + u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t) = \\dfrac{1}{16}e^{-4(t-3)} + \\dfrac{4t-13}{16}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "180acc58-6cf1-560f-ac90-861b9673af03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "180acc58-6cf1-560f-ac90-861b9673af03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315258} :answer-field/id #uuid "03a3d655-9ec1-5074-b9cc-66dad632ea49" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4f34a7a-fc84-5a59-9569-b602aa08ac47" :question/math-academy-id "q-342263" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{2}(t)=u(t-2)$ is the unit step function.\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>1$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=2$ and $f(t)={e}^{t-2}.$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed exponential forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-2)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{{e}^{(t+2)-2}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot L{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{2}(t)\\cdot {e}^{t-2}} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{s-1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-2s}}{(s-1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed252518-aeda-53dc-a900-ce6981c78c16" :knowledge-point/title "Laplace Transforms of First-Order Linear ODEs With Time-Delayed (Discontinuous) Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5cce2c3-c588-5be6-adc1-bedf079c277a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5cce2c3-c588-5be6-adc1-bedf079c277a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316231} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb6351e9-8beb-53ac-b1c7-523b43971f65" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e5fbd03e-952d-5ca5-b272-4b87d3810500" :question/math-academy-id "q-342267" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+y={u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\\,,\\,y(0)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{\\pi }(t)=u(t-\\pi )$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0\n$$\n\n$Y(s)=$\n{{field-1}},$\\,s>0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}+y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} \\\\ L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a derivative and the initial condition $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}=sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s)\n$$\n\nTo find the transform of the right-hand side, we note that the term \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )\n$$\n\nis in the form $u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)$ where $a=\\pi $ and $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi ).$ This can be interpreted as time-delayed sinusoidal forcing. \n\nNow, recall that the second shifting theorem (time-shifting property) states\n\n$$\nL{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t)}={e}^{-as}\\cdot L{f(t+a)}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, by the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{u(t-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡((t+\\pi )-\\pi )} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi s}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute these back into our transformed differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}}+L{y} & =L{{u}_{\\pi }(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\pi )} \\\\ sY(s)+Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ (s+1)Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{{e}^{-\\pi s}}{({s}^{2}+1)(s+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317324 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229172 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f316163e-7e1e-5c7f-a75d-9c917dd8bcda" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Laplace Transform"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "009076e7-87b7-576e-bbb7-5638940d7dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315117} {:answer/id #uuid "032231c3-dc3e-5b5e-8890-15f57b1864de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315134} {:answer/id #uuid "b5480eaa-19d8-5c53-8739-e8b4885f637b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{10-3t}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316145} {:answer/id #uuid "c700e6db-2a5d-50ae-bb39-9ca51c2cb886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}-\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316243} {:answer/id #uuid "e3afda1a-1ec9-56cc-b1c0-d2d20aec3dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "009076e7-87b7-576e-bbb7-5638940d7dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315117} :answer-field/id #uuid "90fe901c-f8c8-565f-93b4-e7e2fe0bf635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d571581-f4a8-5a66-b8eb-3c5c0101beb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342780" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+3y={u}_{3}(t)\\cdot (t-3)\\,,\\,y(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{3}(t)=u(t-3)$ is the unit step function.\n\nYou're given that the Laplace transform $Y(s)=L{y(t)}$ is\n\n$$\nY(s)=-\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{{e}^{-3s}}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}\n$$\n\nand that the solution to the initial value problem can be expressed as \n\n$$\ny(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(t).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-3s}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+3)$ and a repeated linear factor ${s}^{2},$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}(s+3)}=\\frac{A}{s}+\\frac{B}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{C}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\n1=As(s+3)+B(s+3)+C{s}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(3)+C(0)\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Setting $s=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n1=A(0)+B(0)+C(9)\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+C\\,⟹\\,A=-C=-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}{F(s)}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & ={L}^{-1}{-\\frac{1}{9s}+\\frac{1}{3{s}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{9(s+3)}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-3s}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}{{e}^{-as}F(s)}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t-3)f(t-3) & =u(t-3)(-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{t-3}{3}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}) \\\\  & =u(t-3)(\\frac{3t-10}{9}+\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-2{e}^{-3t}+u(t-3)\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{-3(t-3)}+\\frac{3t-10}{9}.\n$$"}]}
